# Background work must become idle

Apply to new or materially changed background work, its producers, and its
startup/lifecycle wiring. This includes `server/src/index.ts`, service workers,
DB connection management, adapters, and plugin or runner background activity.

This is a prospective review requirement, not a demand to migrate every existing
loop. Do not block unrelated edits on untouched polling. Adding a duty to an
existing heartbeat or sweep, extending its lifetime, or copying its polling
pattern is new behavior and must meet this rule. An unchanged adjacent loop is
not precedent or permission.

## Choose the trigger before the mechanism

An idle subsystem must not repeatedly query the database or contact another
service merely to discover that it is still idle. Ask: if nothing changes, is
there a real obligation to act at a future time?

| Obligation | Required approach |
| --- | --- |
| Local state changes or an operation completes | Direct call, event, promise, or completion callback |
| A database write creates work | Transaction-aware notification from the supported producer |
| A routine, expiry, or retry is due later | Register its actual next deadline with the shared scheduler |
| Delivery, cleanup, a claim, or a write remains unresolved | Recovery tied to that concrete obligation and its resolution |
| Work must survive restart | Reconstruct from the existing durable source of truth at startup/resume |

Split mixed duties. Deliver on an event; schedule a retry only after failure or
while an obligation remains. Do not add `setInterval`, recursive timeouts, sleep
loops, or heartbeat callbacks that scan empty work indefinitely. Putting the
same scan behind the scheduler is still polling. A larger interval or `.unref()`
does not establish idle behavior. Configuration or retained completed history
alone must not keep an otherwise idle worker scanning.

## Reuse the shared lifecycle

- Server workers use [delivery-work-coordinator.ts](../server/src/services/delivery-work-coordinator.ts)
  and its [work-scheduler.ts](../server/src/services/work-scheduler.ts) for
  coalescing, non-overlapping execution, retries, and named deadlines. Do not
  create a per-feature scheduler or independent maintenance timer in `index.ts`.
  Adapter, plugin, and runner work uses the owning host's lifecycle interface;
  extend that boundary when needed instead of importing server internals.
- Register, replace, and cancel the actual next deadline. No pending work,
  unresolved write, or future obligation means no scheduled scan. An unconditional
  `nextRunAt = now + interval` is not a deadline unless a real obligation requires it.
- Existing rows remain durable truth; notifications are hints to inspect them.
  Do not add a table per timer or thread recovery bookkeeping through callers.
  Keep work predicates and provider behavior in their owning services, consistent
  with [module-boundaries.mdc](module-boundaries.mdc).
- Use [notifyDeliveryWork](../server/src/services/delivery-work-notifications.ts)
  and [work-signals.ts](../packages/db/src/work-signals.ts) for database-produced
  work. Await intent registration before the relevant write in its transaction.
  A nested savepoint release is not an outer commit. Preserve uncertainty until
  the transaction protocol establishes settlement; one empty query or elapsed
  timeout cannot prove an uncertain write will not commit later.
- Account for every supported producer. Process-local notifications do not cover
  other replicas, independent SQL writers, or external events without ingress.
  Do not remove their only recovery path without replacing that contract.
- Use the shared [database client](../packages/db/src/client.ts) for idle
  connection release and reconnect. Do not add permanent DB keepalives/listeners
  or scattered connect/disconnect wrappers as a substitute for a wake contract.

## Preserve obligations through drain and sleep

A wake is consumed by successful reconciliation, not notification receipt or a
skipped run. Preserve changes arriving during a sweep, a first write during
drain, and failed work whose retry enters drain. Passive `hasPending` checks
must not reintroduce recurring SQL while `run` is paused. Resume must still
process the retained work. Stop subscriptions/timers and cancel or await active
work on shutdown.

The in-process scheduler cannot wake stopped compute. `nextWakeAt()` covers
registered tasks; it is not proof that Cloud has persisted an alarm or that all
work is represented. Before relaxing a sleep blocker, verify the exact durable
wake and admission contract in [idle-sleep-safety.md](../doc/idle-sleep-safety.md).
Preserve unsupported obligations as blockers. Do not change a business deadline
merely because it was inspected, or discard unfinished work to claim quiet.

## Narrow exceptions, with evidence

A bounded readiness probe for an active operation, protocol-required renewal
for a live resource, or reconciliation of an external source with no usable
notification/replay mechanism can need polling. The PR must identify why an
event or deadline is insufficient, the active scope, cadence/backoff, stop
condition, and sleep impact. External-source reconciliation must stop when
unconfigured/disabled; being configured is not by itself an exemption. If its
contract requires activity while otherwise idle, disclose the sleep limitation
and preserve the corresponding blocker until a supported external wake exists.
Required periodic reconciliation still uses the shared scheduler; this exception
does not justify a new independent tenant maintenance interval.

Timers inside the scheduler, timeouts for active requests, and an in-memory
empty-buffer check are not automatically sleep blockers. Assess actual SQL,
network, CPU, and connection effects; do not flag every timer mechanically.
These exceptions do not permit a permanent "just in case" recovery scan.

## Review and verification

Require changes to state the trigger, source of truth, pending-work predicate,
next deadline/retry, and condition that makes the worker quiet. Block added or
expanded idle scans without the narrow exception evidence above. Name the
specific recurring effect and the owning event/deadline alternative in feedback.

For changed behavior, verify no recurring SQL/network calls after startup and
settlement in applicable states: no configuration, configured but idle, and
retained completed history. Prove the first real producer reactivates it. Cover
relevant transaction rollback/outer commit, restart reconstruction, retry,
drain/resume, concurrent wakes, and shutdown boundaries. Select tests for the
changed risks; do not require an unrelated fleet-wide migration or every matrix
case for a small change. Timer-call assertions alone do not prove quiet.

Worker quiet, DB disconnect, and actual host suspension are separate claims.
Do not claim deployed sleep, successful external wake, or cost savings from
unit tests or an internal deadline alone.
